          "unit": "count",
          "kind": "measured",
          "note": "diagnostic_tells: 'A farmyard with eight or more separate small buildings and no single large one.'"
        }
      },
      "variants": [
        {
          "id": "dispersed-single-purpose-cluster",
          "status": "canonical",
          "note": "The farm (tun/gard) is a cluster of many small single-purpose log buildings around a yard: stue (living), stabbur/loft (raised storehouse for grain and textiles), eldhus (baking/brewing, kept separate because it burns), fjos (cattle), stall (horses), smie (smith), badstue (sauna)."
        },
        {
          "id": "single-consolidated-building",
          "status": "forbidden",
          "note": "The swiss-chalet answer to the identical log-length constraint — house, byre and hay store combined in one tall building. Explicitly the opposite strategy: this node's own distinguished_from entry states 'Same construction system, opposite settlement strategy... the Nordic farm disperses into many small buildings around a flat yard.'"
        }
      ],
      "rule": "The farm is a cluster of at least eight small single-purpose log buildings around a yard, not one consolidated house — the direct settlement-level consequence of a wall that cannot be lengthened: a household that needs a new function builds a new building rather than enlarging the old one.",
      "sources": [
        "scandinavian-log-vernacular.json defining_characteristics, diagnostic_tells, distinguished_from"
      ]
